Who Framed Roger Rabbit?

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:04.660
You've seen the movie, you know the story.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:27.340
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:28.860
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.620
And I'm Joe. Who Framed Roger Rabbit?

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.960
You know, the back of the box assumes we've all seen the movie.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.060
So, have we?

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.020
I've seen it like once when I was a kid.

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:43.880
I remember almost nothing about it.

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:45.380
I haven't seen it at all.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:52.300
I mean, I've seen clips, I don't know, when people talk about it with pictures.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.320
So, like, you know, I know, I know what he looks like.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.000
And I know who Jessica Rabbit is.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:00.680
Of course you do.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:02.940
But, yeah, yeah.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.760
But other than that, like, I don't, I don't really know too much.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.300
All right. Well, I do, I love this movie.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.320
I also love Robert Zemeckis.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.220
This is another Robert Zemeckis joint.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:18.720
And also, Robert Zemeckis also made Back to the Future.

00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:25.920
So, I mean, we have two LJN produced movie video games that were both Robert Zemeckis style movies.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:26.760
This is interesting.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:28.800
This is, there's a lot of crossover here.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:36.000
Didn't, wasn't, like, one of the bits of trivia from Back to the Future that Robert Zemeckis hated it?

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.740
Like, hated the video game version?

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:40.880
Of Back to the Future?

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:45.540
Yeah, active, yeah, like, actively hated Back to the Future movie.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:46.940
I'm sorry, game, game.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:48.560
Yeah, Back to the Future, the movie, the game.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:49.520
Oh, well, he probably played it.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.120
Maybe, maybe I'm just misremembering.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:54.440
Yeah, I don't remember that piece of trivia.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:57.720
So, I will try to find a source for that and put it in the show notes if I can.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:58.860
But, that's interesting.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:01.220
I doubt Robert Zemeckis was playing video games.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:07.240
He was pumped. This was his, like, you know, legendary run of movies from, like, 1985 all the way to, uh.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:14.520
Well, I mean, he was, it doesn't, that's not to say that he was playing them, but I'm sure, like, he was aware that a game was being made.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:22.320
Well, maybe he was just mad that so many of his, the games of his movies had nothing to do with the movies themselves.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.160
He's like, no, you don't understand, like, I'm telling a different story.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:30.600
You're telling the story of my characters doing other things that they don't do in my movies.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.720
Yeah, I mean, it also could be that Back to the Future game was just, like, definitively a bad game.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:49.800
Well, the one thing I remember from Who Framed Roger Rabbit, the movie, not the video game that we'll get into in a minute, is that there is a scene in that movie where Bugs Bunny and Mickey Mouse are together in the same shot.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:55.680
And, like, for me, I just had to call that out here on the show because that was, like, a huge, I didn't think that could be done.

00:02:55.700 --> 00:03:05.200
I didn't understand that that was possible, but that's when I learned to love our corporate overlords for giving us the much-needed crossover that now we just get all the time.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.600
Any, uh, any two pop culture characters that we want, put them together.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:17.900
It's a multiverse. I'm still waiting for a live-action Batman and Iron Man movie, so I think that'd be fun.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:19.000
Soon enough.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.960
You know, Marvel vs. DC is only, like, 30 years away.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:24.460
Who Framed Roger Rabbit?

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:35.420
The Game is based on the movie, but maybe not if you ask Robert Zemeckis, but it's a action-adventure game where the player controls private detective Eddie Valiant.

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:45.940
Accompanied by Roger Rabbit, who you do not get to play out of at all, you must find clues and items that help you progress the story and find out, you guessed it,

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:48.500
who framed Roger Rabbit.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:53.120
Sounds like an okay game to me, but didn't you guys want to play as Roger Rabbit?

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.340
Didn't you think you were going to play as Roger Rabbit?

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:56.920
I mean, I don't know.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:04.660
Like I said, I haven't seen the movie in a long time, but isn't, isn't, uh, this other guy, the detective, isn't he, like, the actual main character?

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:10.620
Yes. Okay, but yeah, sure, he's the main character, but this is a children's game now, and, like, you know, you think it...

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.260
Yeah, no, I don't think so.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:13.320
No? I beg to differ there.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.780
Okay. No, I'm just thinking, like, the NES is, like, a children's console, you know, like...

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:18.300
Well, here's the question that I have.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:21.840
Would you rather be Roger Rabbit or hang out with Roger Rabbit?

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.780
Like, it's not, I don't think that, like, Roger Rabbit is, uh, is like, oh, men want to be him.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:34.820
Um, but he might be fun to hang out with, and that's exactly what this is simulating here.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:37.900
He just sort of, like, follows you around, and what?

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:40.380
I'll tell you why, because think about it like this.

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:44.300
Would you rather be Sonic the Hedgehog or hang out with Sonic the Hedgehog?

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:45.480
Sonic the Hedgehog does not translate to Roger Rabbit.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:47.560
No, no, no, listen. It does not translate.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:53.700
Listen, one fan made a game where you just hang out with Sonic the Hedgehog in your apartment.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:57.640
That's true. And he progressively does creepier and creepier things to you.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:00.500
And that's what you just made me think of with Roger Rabbit.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:12.940
I'd rather play as him in some wacky platformer that is inspired by, like, the, um, the opening short with him and the baby who turns out to be, like, a 40-year-old man.

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:15.620
Um, I don't know if you guys, you guys haven't seen the movie, so it doesn't matter.

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:26.260
But that's, like, the opening sketch is, like, oh, Roger Rabbit is actually, like, a famous cartoon character in this universe, and he makes the movies in the cartoons in this toon world.

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:31.940
And I thought, like, you know, you could do that as, like, a platformer or, like, a Bugs Bunny's Crazy Castle-style game.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:36.700
But instead, they decided to take the route that the movie totally takes, so it's fair.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:47.400
I just don't know how much fun it is to be the private detective and then just have Roger Rabbit follow you around, but not even, like, closely follow you.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:52.660
He just kind of has his own mind and doesn't really interact with much of anything.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:59.560
Well, here's where I want to just kind of, uh, argue my point here.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:05.840
Um, the difference between Roger Rabbit and Sonic is that Sonic has, like, you know, he's fast.

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:06.740
He has powers.

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:07.740
He does things.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:20.280
Whereas Roger Rabbit, as far as I can tell, as somebody that has mostly just absorbed knowledge of Who Framed Roger Rabbit through, like, I don't know, pop culture retrospectives.

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:26.080
Um, it seems like he's just, like, a dude, like a dude cartoon and, like, more like an actor.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:27.480
Is that the case?

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:32.680
Uh, yes to the actor, but I wouldn't say he's just, like, a guy living in that universe.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:42.200
He just takes on, like, the trope of, uh, you know, a Bugs Bunny, a Mickey Mouse, a Woody Woodpecker, just, like, the lead person who can do it all.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:44.500
Okay. I, yeah, I don't know.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:52.240
I guess if we're going for a mystery and solving it, I would probably go with, like, the actual detective.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:58.440
Unless it was called Detective Roger Rabbit, which might be another thing in an alternate universe.

00:06:58.700 --> 00:07:05.160
Well, so I'm thinking of, like, what I would have, how I would have reacted as a kid in 1989 when this came out.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:13.960
And I feel like, yes, if we were talking, like, a platformer or something, I'd want to be, like, the colorful, you know, crazy, cartoony Roger Rabbit.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:19.280
But, like, my, you know, my, like, eight-year-old brain would be, like, oh, like, I want to be the, I want to be the detective.

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:25.460
Like, I want to be this cool guy and, like, have to protect the, like, goofy cartoon character because I'm too cool for cartoons.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:33.540
Like, you know, I feel like I would be, you know, for that age, you might, you might actually benefit from not being the cartoon character.

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:59.300
So they tried to make it true to the story of the movie, like, finding clues to figure out who framed Roger Rabbit.

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:02.960
As a detective in this game, how do you find these clues?

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:06.100
Because, remember, this isn't Rockstar's L.A.

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:15.500
Noire. You're not going around and interrogating people and doing, like, you know, custom speech dialogue to get proper answers and stuff like that.

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:36.400
Mike? This is a game where, ultimately, the detective part of it, like, the mystery-solving part, to me, I don't even know where to start because it's so, it's just so bizarre for a child's video game based on a hit movie to take the most, you know, the hardest route to getting around to the answers.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:39.180
Mike? Can you help me?

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:41.640
No. There is nothing in this building.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:45.560
I didn't know what you were doing for a second there.

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:53.840
I was like, is Sean okay? It's so, like, humiliating, being a detective in this game.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:56.960
You're just like, oh, can you help me?

00:08:57.960 --> 00:08:59.660
And that's all you ask.

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:00.520
That's all you can ask.

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:02.740
And there's, like, there's nothing in this building.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:04.840
I was like, check the drawers, please.

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:08.800
Like, that's what being a detective is like in this game.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:13.300
And then you find stuff just by sheer brute force, pretty much.

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:17.320
That's what my experience was, at least.

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:21.780
Yeah, but at least you only have to wait, like, 11 seconds while you search each.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:25.460
Well, if you want to search the desk, yeah, you'll search for 11 seconds.

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:28.180
You gotta just sit there. Well, it's very realistic, yes.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:42.340
So, for those who don't know, what you do is you, as the detective, are on the streets of Toontown, and you go into a building, and you immediately, like, find a person or a stray cat that wants to kill you.

00:09:42.640 --> 00:09:44.240
But say you find the person first.

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:47.560
You press the A button only once.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:53.260
Don't you dare press it twice, or you will cancel the dialogue option between you and the other NPC.

00:09:53.260 --> 00:10:01.320
However, if you do manage to have the conversation, it is just what Sean asked me earlier, and there are only three possible answers.

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:06.060
There's either the building is empty, the building has something, or search the drawers.

00:10:06.340 --> 00:10:09.780
And that might prove to be, like, okay, great.

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:11.640
Like, that's how you find out.

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:16.660
That's a valid strategy for finding out if there's anything worth your time in these buildings.

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:34.780
But every single time the game resets which buildings have which items and where they are, the only way to ever play this game from, you know, from start to finish is that you must go into every building, and at least you could just, I guess, not ask anybody and just explore everything.

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:39.660
But you have to ask at least one person in each building, hey, is there anything in here?

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:42.360
And they just say no. It's like, what is the point of that?

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:49.160
Why randomize everything if it just comes down to a simple, like, speech test against the NPCs?

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:53.780
Look, I'll say that, you know, on the surface, well, first of all, yes, I agree.

00:10:53.900 --> 00:10:55.480
None of this is executed well at all.

00:10:56.420 --> 00:11:02.040
It's very, it's very, the gameplay loop when it comes to this part is not fun.

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:04.140
Just, there's no other way to put it.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:12.740
But I'll say that on the surface, or on paper, rather, I get, like, I can see a world where this is okay, because it's just a means to get you around town.

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:15.140
It's like a little bit of a MacGuffin or whatever you want to call it.

00:11:15.400 --> 00:11:21.900
Like, that's just like, yeah, this is how, this is what gets you into building a building where, like, other crap happens that's, like, exciting or whatever.

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:26.300
Like, things will attack you or you gotta answer these dumb jokes or whatever.

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:31.000
But it's like, I get that as being like, well, this gameplay loop could work.

00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:33.580
It needs to be contextualized.

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:35.160
Everything else worked perfectly with it.

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:40.000
And it wasn't quite so, like, it wasn't quite so tedious.

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:44.700
Like, maybe have, like, one or two things to search in each building if you're gonna do it this way.

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:46.420
Just let you search it.

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:49.340
You don't have to wait for realism's sake.

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:58.320
Like, and then you have to definitely have, like, enough exciting things going on between that that makes, like, the search feel exciting.

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:03.960
Yeah, I mean, right now, what we're playing in this game is the template.

00:12:04.220 --> 00:12:08.900
Like, there's no reason why anything is in any building.

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:12.700
There's no context.

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:16.400
There's no dramatic force.

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:21.320
Like, we're playing the how-to.

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:23.880
It's like, hi, my name is Love Interest.

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:27.120
And I'm going to guide you to plot point two.

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:31.900
And that's exactly like, if this game were the story, that's how it would be written out.

00:12:31.900 --> 00:12:37.180
And that means that it's all, like, I felt like what I was doing was completely useless.

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:42.200
Because there was nothing mysterious about this detective story.

00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:46.260
It was just, oh, it's just, I have to click on everything, pretty much.

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:48.520
And that's how you solve things.

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:58.260
Right, yeah. I mean, to follow up on what Joe was saying, if they said that there was something in the building, so that you knew, like, okay, yeah, this is why they randomized it.

00:12:58.260 --> 00:13:01.060
Because sometimes the build, and every building is completely different, right?

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:02.260
Not the case in this game.

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:05.080
But let's pretend in a different game, every building will be different.

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:10.080
So you never know which challenging thing you're going to have to get X item in Y building.

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:12.760
Because you don't want to deal with that challenge this time.

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:14.080
Time for a reset of your game.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:19.780
Instead, once you find out that there's something in the building, it's just on the freaking floor.

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:21.540
And you pick it up and you leave.

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:28.840
It's not even like there's some kind of challenge or things specific to only that building or a location or something like that.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:30.680
Or a puzzle. Even when they tell you, yeah, exactly.

00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:36.620
When they tell you, search the drawers or search around or whatever, I was thinking, like, oh, there's this little mouse hole.

00:13:36.800 --> 00:13:38.480
Like, maybe there's something in there.

00:13:38.520 --> 00:13:41.420
And I was thinking, like, oh, I'm thinking ahead of the game.

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:43.300
Like, they designed that really well.

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:46.900
It's noticeable. I'm going to search in the mouse hole and something funny is going to happen.

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:48.940
And it's just, there's nothing here.

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:51.160
Well, then don't even make it an option then.

00:13:52.460 --> 00:14:01.280
Each interactable object in this game is, like, a less interesting equivalent of flushable toilets in, like, first-person shooters.

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:02.620
It's just, like, there's there.

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:06.200
And it's there to, like, make you feel like the world is interactive.

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:12.040
But it's really just a cookie cutter, like, button press.

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:14.200
I don't know. I mean, it's very sad.

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:15.300
But to be clear, things can.

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:16.600
It's possible.

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:20.000
Things are sometimes hidden in those mouse holes or in the drawers.

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:22.500
It's just way more often than just sitting on the floor.

00:14:22.620 --> 00:14:29.340
And there's way too many mouse holes and drawers and desks that have nothing in it that just make it all so pointless.

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:33.400
And, yeah, I think, I mean, I feel like I want to make this suggestion.

00:14:33.400 --> 00:14:38.500
But I feel like it's one of those things where it's like, well, yeah, you're just talking about making a whole different game now.

00:14:38.660 --> 00:14:46.420
But, like, my thought is, like, if these rooms were a little more puzzly or a little more, like, contextual.

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:52.420
It's like, yeah, the baseball is in, like, a sporting goods store or something.

00:14:52.600 --> 00:15:00.920
You know, whatever. But, like, I'm thinking of it like you can make these, like, tiny little mini escape rooms where there's, like, a little puzzle or a little something you've got to figure out.

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:04.980
Rather than just being totally random.

00:15:06.020 --> 00:15:07.260
You know, it's like Spy vs.

00:15:07.420 --> 00:15:13.180
Spy without the element of, like, the two-player, like, I wonder where he hid the trap thing.

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:15.120
It's just things are just randomly placed.

00:15:15.140 --> 00:15:17.740
And that takes away what I think makes Spy vs.

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:19.560
Spy cool. Yeah.

00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:40.600
And that's, like, when this game describes itself as an adventure game, that's what, like, that's what comes to mind is are these really ridiculous ways to interact with the world in very specific ways that, while it may not make sense, you may need to buy, like, a fake mustache to get something out of a drain pipe or something.

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:47.080
But at least there's something going on instead of, like, oh, let's just check all the drawers.

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:49.180
Like, let's just brute force this.

00:15:50.220 --> 00:16:03.580
Yeah. And for a place like Toontown, the apartment buildings are, like, all exactly the same and really boring just consisting of desks and drawers that you can search through and everybody's rooms are just, like, their bedroom.

00:16:03.880 --> 00:16:06.120
There's no, like, wacky, zany things.

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:12.040
And yet the actual gameplay parts of it do include some cartoony-like elements.

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:26.620
I noticed that if you hit a person and then talk to them, not all the times, but sometimes they will respond, go away, you horrid man, because they're reacting to the fact that you just beat them up for no reason.

00:16:27.460 --> 00:16:29.360
Reactivity. Yeah, exactly.

00:16:29.560 --> 00:16:34.480
It's like, that's a nice touch for a game to have like this that otherwise has zero personality.

00:16:34.720 --> 00:16:38.540
Think about when you're walking along the streets to get from building to building.

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:47.480
If you get run over by one of the cars, you get, like, you know, laid out like one of those bear rugs, you know, you or Roger.

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:52.160
Now, Roger, if he gets hit, he's fine because he's just a cartoon.

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:55.660
So he just, like, unflattens himself and everything's back to normal.

00:16:55.900 --> 00:17:01.660
If you get hit by a car, you're a human being with, you know, vital organs that are no longer intact.

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:06.740
You know, like, I assume that just they've got a lot of little, like, just slapstick moments for Roger Rabbit.

00:17:06.740 --> 00:17:08.040
They're like, that's what he's there for.

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:18.040
And also, like, you're there to, like, maybe I'm misremembering the movie, but, like, maybe, or, like, does this make sense that you're kind of there to protect Roger?

00:17:19.120 --> 00:17:19.980
Yes. Yeah.

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:22.280
You hate cartoons.

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:24.660
Okay. That's the whole idea.

00:17:24.780 --> 00:17:26.360
Like, the detective hates cartoons.

00:17:26.360 --> 00:17:37.400
He, I think his, I think the story, which I'm now slightly misremembering too, but I believe his, like, buddy cop was killed by a toon or something like that.

00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:39.860
And that's why he doesn't trust cartoon characters anymore.

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:45.200
Wait, so he begrudgingly, we're just talking about the movie now.

00:17:45.340 --> 00:17:49.300
So he just begrudgingly accepts Roger Rabbit's request for help?

00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:51.780
Well, because now he's a private detective.

00:17:51.780 --> 00:17:56.100
So, you know, it's like before he used to be on The Force and everything.

00:17:56.360 --> 00:17:58.060
It's all the noir tropes, you know?

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:03.580
Yeah, yeah. But I don't think he is, I don't think Roger Rabbit, like, comes to him.

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:05.920
I don't recall exactly who comes to him.

00:18:05.960 --> 00:18:07.140
It might have been Jessica Rabbit.

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:08.260
Oh, I see.

00:18:08.340 --> 00:18:09.200
Okay. Yeah.

00:18:14.480 --> 00:18:18.680
Anyway, speaking of just cartoon-y things that happen in this game,

00:18:18.680 --> 00:18:30.600
occasionally as you're wandering around town, a group of Judge Doom's weasel henchmen will come up and potentially grab Roger and hold him hostage.

00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:36.540
And the only way to free him is to successfully tell the punchline to their jokes.

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:44.140
And I can't help but feel like this is one of the best moments in the game, no matter how painful the punchlines actually are.

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:50.880
Yeah, it's definitely a different form of combat, if you want to call it that.

00:18:51.060 --> 00:19:00.960
This game does have combat, too, but I like to think of it more as a creative solution to fights.

00:19:02.020 --> 00:19:05.480
Yeah, it's a nice, it's a nice interrupter.

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:09.000
That's one thing I will say, this game is going, but it's got a lot of variety.

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:13.020
And it's kind of, it is funny to see all these really bad jokes come up.

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:17.860
But, and I know we keep bringing it back to the movie, but does something like that happen in the movie,

00:19:17.880 --> 00:19:20.760
where, like, they laugh so hard that everyone gets away or something?

00:19:20.780 --> 00:19:21.800
Or is this just random?

00:19:23.400 --> 00:19:25.380
I'm pretty sure that happens in the movie, yes.

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:27.920
The weasels are, yeah, like, known for laughing.

00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:36.580
But I would even just argue that, like, that kind of, you know, very on-the-nose silliness is what the movie is known for, too.

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:41.540
And I feel like here it's executed really well, because it's also, it's not just, you know,

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:45.220
here's an A, B, C, D thing, and you just pick one of the options.

00:19:45.220 --> 00:19:50.060
You have to, like, cycle through potential answers and then select the one that you think matches their joke.

00:19:50.060 --> 00:19:59.100
And it just feels like it's an actual interactive moment rather than just, like, a forced cutscene that only happens, like, one time.

00:19:59.120 --> 00:20:00.960
And, you know, the joke is obvious.

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:07.940
You gotta use your allotted tune seconds very thoughtfully.

00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:10.780
They call them tune seconds.

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:11.960
Yeah, what was that?

00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:14.880
I thought, when I read that in the manual, it says nine tune seconds.

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:17.960
I thought they were trying to make a really bad play on 19.

00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:19.920
Oh, my God. But it's not 19.

00:20:19.920 --> 00:20:20.780
It's nine seconds.

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:22.480
I don't know.

00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:24.660
I just, I laughed at that.

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:26.160
And I'm not sure it was different.

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:29.840
Well, you see, movies are filmed in frames per second.

00:20:30.140 --> 00:20:34.620
But because this was an animated movie, it was filmed in tunes per second.

00:20:34.620 --> 00:20:36.480
So that was a reference to the movie as well.

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:41.940
That sounds contrived, but I can't tell if you're messing with me.

00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:43.240
I can't tell if it's made up or not either.

00:20:43.480 --> 00:20:44.340
So I'm just not reacting.

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:52.140
I want to talk about the strange selection of both user interface and inputs to do things in this game, though.

00:20:52.340 --> 00:20:56.660
Because we were just talking about the weasel punchline thing, which I thought was done very well.

00:20:56.800 --> 00:21:07.040
Except for I didn't know how to even see any punchlines until I realized you have to hold down select and then cycle with the D-pad through the options.

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:10.040
It's like, why wouldn't the D-pad just work by itself?

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:11.560
Like, why would you have to hold select?

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:21.920
The choice of text on the menu screens at all time of like, you know, like your lives are represented by these clapboards.

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:26.120
And your power is these little P buttons, P icons.

00:21:26.380 --> 00:21:31.100
And then your strength meter is just always on the screen, even if there's like no combat necessary.

00:21:31.100 --> 00:21:37.360
But it's something that winds up like a meter for, you know, for like a golf swing.

00:21:37.540 --> 00:21:44.300
It's very just a strange choice of of menu design elements that are just constantly around you.

00:21:44.300 --> 00:21:50.740
Yeah, and a ton of a ton of real estate on that on that menu at the top is given to like just the words Roger Rabbit.

00:21:50.980 --> 00:22:02.500
And the fact that like some of them have have multiple uses and like because the power meter is also where it tells you what you're doing.

00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:13.180
And while I think it makes sense and it's a it's a good like workaround to have like your usable items be cycled through with like the select button.

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:24.260
I have no idea why in a screen where you have no other like anything to input for, you still have to do that in the joke phase.

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:32.700
I that was that was odd, but I don't think that an expert in UI UX was on staff.

00:22:33.320 --> 00:22:36.860
Yeah, well, I also I feel like the select thing.

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:40.160
Because that was really just baffling to me, too.

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:45.100
I had to look it up like how to how to change the punchlines as well.

00:22:45.220 --> 00:22:50.360
And it feels like, well, you know, the the D pad is the is the most is the best way to use this.

00:22:50.360 --> 00:22:56.680
But like this button's called select, so we have to use that somehow or else people are going to take everything literally.

00:22:57.220 --> 00:23:01.400
Yeah, like I feel like it's like you just because it's called select doesn't mean it has to be the button.

00:23:01.560 --> 00:23:03.900
You have to somehow incorporate it into selecting things.

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:05.480
And I just want to throw it out there.

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:06.820
You know, we didn't mention it.

00:23:06.920 --> 00:23:12.420
We did throw LJN under the bus, but they actually hired rare to develop this game.

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:24.440
So this is kind of unusual that we're, you know, we're complaining so much about normal gameplay design elements from a company that usually we enjoy the way they think outside of the box.

00:23:25.580 --> 00:23:27.980
I think that's a thing with LJN, too, though.

00:23:28.120 --> 00:23:39.120
Like what I associate them with are like this this really weird dude that just like, oh, we're going to make it so that you can drive a car and you have full 360 control steering.

00:23:39.120 --> 00:23:44.900
But we're not going to let you off the train tracks and we're going to have Roger Rabbit follow you around.

00:23:45.020 --> 00:23:46.120
But like not really close.

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:47.340
And it's not going to make a lot of sense.

00:23:47.460 --> 00:23:57.880
He's just going to kind of walk like that's sort of what they've been doing with whether it's whether it's Jaws or like all the line of bullies that you have to throw.

00:23:57.880 --> 00:24:03.260
You have to throw like beer bottles at and in in back to the future.

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:06.340
Like they're just wild and crazy ideas.

00:24:06.440 --> 00:24:13.560
They just throw stuff at the wall and it's kind of endearing that they that they have these unique ideas.

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:16.200
It's just they never really think too much about them.

00:24:16.720 --> 00:24:19.120
So that's what I think of LJN.

00:24:19.660 --> 00:24:30.440
And this is still like we've talked about before, like Rare doesn't really have they haven't really proven that they have the kind of pedigree that we associate with Rare yet.

00:24:30.640 --> 00:24:35.140
So I this this is sort of like par for the course, but them, too.

00:24:35.280 --> 00:24:44.300
Right. Well, I guess I would just say that every time they've given us a game that wasn't just like a Jeopardy Wheel of Fortune thing, it's a completely different game.

00:24:44.300 --> 00:24:46.800
And this this one follows that rule as well.

00:24:46.940 --> 00:24:56.620
You know, who framed Roger Rabbit for me personally is so hard to talk about because it's unlike any of the other 222 NES games we played before it.

00:24:56.940 --> 00:25:07.560
However, I would just say that, like, while I'm not a huge fan of something like Wizards and Warriors or Slalom and stuff like that, those games are good.

00:25:07.720 --> 00:25:09.700
You know, like they're technically impressive for the time.

00:25:09.700 --> 00:25:12.900
And I had very little to complain about this game.

00:25:12.900 --> 00:25:18.660
It feels like it's more like you were saying an LJN pitch them like you're going to make this game for us.

00:25:18.740 --> 00:25:19.760
And here's how it's going to work.

00:25:19.960 --> 00:25:23.640
And, you know, Rare was forced to make it the way LJN wanted it.

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:25.080
That's the only sense I could make of it.

00:25:25.700 --> 00:25:26.880
Yeah, make it follows.

00:25:28.100 --> 00:25:30.500
This definitely feels more LJN than Rare.

00:25:31.340 --> 00:25:36.400
Yeah. And you mentioned, like, their, their, like, ambition being kind of endearing.

00:25:36.700 --> 00:25:49.620
And I, like, really agree with that because, like, I, I enjoyed playing this game just purely out of, like, oh, they tried to do this and they tried to do that.

00:25:49.620 --> 00:26:03.380
You know, like, even when a lot of it doesn't work, even when, like, if I take away my, like, just my, like, the intrigue that I have about, like, what they were attempting to do, then, like, if I take away that, a lot of these things, yeah, are not fun.

00:26:03.380 --> 00:26:13.000
But, like, I just, I just appreciate seeing all of these, like, all of these, like, things that they try to do in the systems that they use to try to make it work.

00:26:13.120 --> 00:26:14.240
Like, it's kind of cool.

00:26:14.620 --> 00:26:21.940
If you're, if you're, if you're interested in, like, in old games and stuff, I feel like this is just, it's just fun to kind of, like, break this game down.

00:26:22.260 --> 00:26:25.140
Yeah, and understand what's going on, going on under the hood.

00:26:25.900 --> 00:26:30.740
And also just, like, the, the fact that bad is so much better than bland.

00:26:30.740 --> 00:26:44.960
So, um, a lot of the games that, uh, Mike mentioned, uh, that Rare had done previously, like, yeah, they're definitely competent games, but I don't really, they don't really occupy too much space in my head.

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:50.880
Um, whereas I feel like I'm not going to forget playing Who Framed Roger Rabbit because it's so weird.

00:26:51.400 --> 00:26:52.440
So, yeah. Yeah.

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:15.120
Let's go over the points of progression throughout this game.

00:27:15.120 --> 00:27:27.660
Because you're a detective trying to get to the bottom of this, the whole game is just about finding series of clues to unlock the next gated part of the game to then allow you to move forward.

00:27:27.900 --> 00:27:37.540
So, the first thing you have to do is, and you will find many other things along the way, of which I can't tell you what you might specifically find because it'll be, uh, unique to your, uh, playthrough.

00:27:37.540 --> 00:27:43.700
But the first thing you need to do is find the password and the rose to get into the ink and paint club.

00:27:43.980 --> 00:27:46.360
Now, you can just find just the password to get in.

00:27:46.540 --> 00:27:53.640
However, Jessica Rabbit won't talk to you unless you give her a rose because that's how she knows that you love her or something, I suppose.

00:27:54.180 --> 00:27:55.560
So, you give her the rose.

00:27:55.740 --> 00:27:57.260
Now you can talk to Jessica Rabbit.

00:27:57.640 --> 00:28:00.820
She'll give you clues to where the will is.

00:28:00.820 --> 00:28:06.360
And now the game becomes no longer about finding items, but finding pieces of the will.

00:28:06.580 --> 00:28:12.060
So, you locate the pieces of the will based on the obscure hints she gives you about where the will might be.

00:28:12.140 --> 00:28:14.380
And this is a little bit where the game opens up.

00:28:14.500 --> 00:28:23.320
But ultimately, it's just another batch of, okay, now instead of going into buildings and asking, is there anything in here?

00:28:23.420 --> 00:28:27.960
Now you're asking Jessica Rabbit, uh, do you have any idea where the next piece of your will is?

00:28:29.680 --> 00:28:32.620
And so, I don't know if you guys want to talk about, like, you know, that part of the game.

00:28:32.780 --> 00:28:37.000
But, like, for me, it just, it didn't actually feel like anything opened up.

00:28:37.120 --> 00:28:43.140
You know, they try to make it so, like, now you can go into the woods and go into the caves and do this and that to find the will.

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:45.940
And it's like, I don't know, it didn't really actually seem any more exciting.

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:54.940
I bet for them it was, like, that moment at the end of Final Fantasy VII, at the beginning of the end of Final Fantasy VII, where you leave Midgard.

00:28:54.940 --> 00:28:56.180
And it's like, now you've got the whole world.

00:28:56.200 --> 00:28:58.940
And it's like, maybe that's what they thought Roger Rabbit was going to be like.

00:28:59.080 --> 00:29:00.760
Now you can explore outside the buildings.

00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:07.260
No, because it doesn't change the fact that we're still just playing the bones of, like, a story.

00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:11.080
Like, they're referring to these things as pieces of the will.

00:29:11.200 --> 00:29:14.640
But it's just, you need to collect three key items.

00:29:14.920 --> 00:29:19.000
And, like, you have to go back and ask her, like, where's the next key item, please?

00:29:19.340 --> 00:29:20.460
And she'll tell you.

00:29:20.460 --> 00:29:23.260
And then, like, that's sort of what's going on.

00:29:23.440 --> 00:29:27.880
It doesn't feel like you're opening up because, again, there's no context to anything.

00:29:28.440 --> 00:29:32.560
Yeah. And, like, you leave the city and you go into caves.

00:29:32.740 --> 00:29:39.360
And that's a little different because, like you said, instead of asking people what's here, you have to, like, fight a snake or something in the cave.

00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:46.620
And then you'll need, like, items, like you might have to get, like, the spring shoes that you would have had to find somewhere else to jump over, like, a chasm or something.

00:29:48.340 --> 00:29:51.640
But then that's the new gameplay loop.

00:29:51.800 --> 00:29:53.740
Like, that one change happens.

00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:57.400
And then you do that 40 times, like, looking for things.

00:29:57.400 --> 00:30:01.740
And you go and talk to Jessica Rabbit and she gives you the clue and everything.

00:30:01.900 --> 00:30:07.780
But it's, like, yeah, there's, like, a minor tweak to the gameplay loop that just, like, incorporates into the gameplay loop a little bit.

00:30:08.280 --> 00:30:11.380
But still becomes very repetitive along with everything else.

00:30:13.940 --> 00:30:20.760
In a very, like, basic way, a lot of these early games attempt to be nonlinear.

00:30:21.120 --> 00:30:26.020
Like, I think there is a very obvious points of progression thing that I just laid out.

00:30:26.140 --> 00:30:30.940
But if you're a kid exploring this game, those things aren't incredibly obvious to you.

00:30:31.120 --> 00:30:33.940
I'm looking on game FAQs to figure out, like, what to do next.

00:30:33.940 --> 00:30:41.160
There was this whole, like, nonlinear gameplay for a lot of these action-adventure NES games.

00:30:41.340 --> 00:30:43.680
And then we just kind of said, like, that's not working.

00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:53.240
And the Super Nintendo, the N64, like, for a while it was just, like, linear games that were very, like, follow-the-hallway style stuff.

00:30:53.240 --> 00:31:01.620
And now we're getting back to the whole, like, open world, do-whatever-you-want style things that, like, yeah, these games aren't open world.

00:31:01.620 --> 00:31:04.040
I'm not making an argument for who framed Roger Rabbit there.

00:31:04.160 --> 00:31:11.500
But I'm saying it is funny that games tried to do something very early on that they just didn't have the technology ready for yet.

00:31:11.500 --> 00:31:27.400
And I think that's the problem here is that Rare should have found a better way to communicate, like, what the new gameplay flow was rather than have it be just checking in with the same person over and over again to figure out what's the next place I have to go to.

00:31:27.400 --> 00:31:37.280
You know, think about, like, Ninja Gaiden does it through, like, cutscenes, right, of giving you the next bits of the story and then you just play the exciting moments.

00:31:37.280 --> 00:31:54.200
I think Hoover and Roger Rabbit didn't need to be level-based, but it certainly needed to maybe have a moment of locked, you know, cutscene footage where you see, like, the next moment of the story unfold and understand, like, oh, okay, so now I gotta go here, you know?

00:31:54.200 --> 00:32:01.940
It would have been a little more exciting, I feel like, but they chose to make a game where you can find anything in any particular order because every time it's different.

00:32:02.180 --> 00:32:03.940
Yeah, I think that the...

00:32:03.940 --> 00:32:08.580
It's more open-ended than it is open world, so yeah, you're right on that count.

00:32:09.180 --> 00:32:22.040
But I think that this game could have made a lot more sense and they would have the ability to flesh things out a bit more if they just did away with the whole randomization.

00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:42.200
Like, you could have finding the baseball in the sporting goods store even, like, yeah, just because it would be a hand-placed item that you can put, like, little environmental details in instead of everything being, like, a hotel hallway that people are walking around with desks and tables.

00:32:42.200 --> 00:32:51.560
Like, there would be more of an opportunity to flesh this world out because you didn't have to worry about everything being interchangeable.

00:32:51.780 --> 00:33:04.700
And I guess that's a lesson that they're learning here, or hopefully they learned it, the industry is learning here, that unless you have unlimited resources that this game definitely didn't have,

00:33:06.160 --> 00:33:11.660
the wider you make something, the shallower it becomes, and this is a prime example of that.

00:33:12.200 --> 00:33:17.840
Yeah, and, like, to me it's kind of the equivalent to what we always say about enemy placement.

00:33:18.600 --> 00:33:29.740
Where it's like, they're doing the same problem that we have with a lot of games where, like, enemies just randomly appear anywhere, and it doesn't always end up being fun because it wasn't designed specifically to be fun.

00:33:30.240 --> 00:33:32.040
Whereas, like, this is the same thing.

00:33:32.040 --> 00:33:38.980
Like, in this case, if the developers took a little more control over where things were, it could feel a little more intentional.

00:33:38.980 --> 00:33:46.540
It could feel a little more like you solved a puzzle rather than, like, you just looked everywhere because you knew the game was just, like, randomly generating these items.

00:33:47.000 --> 00:33:55.620
So, like, it's weird because I feel like we've not really – maybe we have seen things that have the same problem, but we've not really talked about that problem outside of, like, enemy placement.

00:33:55.620 --> 00:33:59.540
But it's kind of giving me the same vibe.

00:33:59.760 --> 00:34:08.340
And that's why I'll say that, like, I don't even necessarily think that this type of game doesn't work because the technology is not there.

00:34:08.360 --> 00:34:14.120
But I think that it's because the – yeah, the gaming industry's experience isn't there yet.

00:34:14.120 --> 00:34:20.840
Like, there's not enough gaming history to draw on and, like, learn from the mistakes of older games.

00:34:21.560 --> 00:34:30.200
Whereas, like, you know, maybe a decade down the line or whatever when, like, other games are coming out, like GTA and stuff's coming out and things like that.

00:34:30.260 --> 00:34:36.900
It's like, oh, we've kind of – we have a good idea of what works and what doesn't and how to kind of make this kind of thing work.

00:34:37.040 --> 00:34:43.400
We keep mentioning that inside these buildings or just locations you go to that there are items you pick up.

00:34:43.400 --> 00:34:46.600
There are a lot of different items to pick up.

00:34:46.780 --> 00:34:48.000
There are wallets.

00:34:48.360 --> 00:34:49.560
There's whistles.

00:34:50.580 --> 00:34:51.880
There's custard pies.

00:34:52.900 --> 00:34:54.120
You know, there's a lot of stuff.

00:34:54.120 --> 00:35:00.360
So I don't want to, like, break down the power-ups, if you want to call them that, you know, at a time.

00:35:00.440 --> 00:35:03.440
Was there anything you guys specifically thought was pretty unique?

00:35:03.880 --> 00:35:10.740
I'll take the obvious one here with the whistle being able to use to hail Benny the Cab so that you could travel the map faster.

00:35:10.740 --> 00:35:20.920
That felt like an actual – a little bit ahead of its time but only because we haven't had all those Japanese RPGs come over yet.

00:35:21.040 --> 00:35:23.460
You know, they clearly took it from, like, those kinds of overall games.

00:35:23.460 --> 00:35:32.160
But that feels like you're getting the airship moment where you can now explore the map a lot faster and see sections you didn't think you could see before.

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:33.860
Or I like stuff like that.

00:35:34.060 --> 00:35:40.120
I'm less jazzed about, like, using a wallet to purchase items at a store.

00:35:40.360 --> 00:35:44.820
It's like, I have to give the whole wallet away just to buy things at the store.

00:35:44.820 --> 00:35:49.740
I mean, I like that there's different classes of things.

00:35:49.920 --> 00:35:52.560
Like, yeah, there's currency, which is just wallets.

00:35:52.700 --> 00:35:59.920
Like, there's no money in them, but this version of Los Angeles trades exclusively in wallets.

